      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-225727",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/55/5596a286f89b2eed9fd6340c00e77915f84883f2d57d1b342b762143811537aa.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n = 64$ from a population of size $N = 500$. Given that the unbiased estimates of the population mean $\u03bc$ and population variance $\u03c3^{2}$ from this sample are $x^(\u2015) = 87.35$ and $s^{2} = 6.7^{2}$, respectively, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.656) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $63$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\nThe required confidence interval is\n{{field-1}}\n$\u00b1$\n{{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
