      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251237",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/b9/b931d51022ec1fe4b1fbb9bb5318eae62bb87ac4c99a016af74a52b63ce1743a.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n = 28$ from a normally distributed population of size $N = 300$. Given that the unbiased estimates of the population mean $\u03bc$ and population variance $\u03c3^{2}$ from this sample are $x^(\u2015) = 6.5$ and $s^{2} = 2.25^{2}$, respectively, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.052) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $27$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\nThe required confidence interval is\n{{field-1}}\n$\u00b1$\n{{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
